'''''Phleum bertolonii''''' ('''smaller cat's-tail''') is a species of [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[Poaceae|grass]] native to most of Europe, southwestern Asia, and northwestern Africa.<ref name="POWO"/><ref name="PlantAtlas">{{cite web |title=Smaller Cat's-tail ''Phleum bertolonii'' DC. |website=PlantAtlas |url=https://plantatlas2020.org/atlas/2cd4p9h.fw8 |access-date=2026-03-14}}</ref> It is a member of the genus ''[[Phleum]]'', which consists of 17 species of annual and perennial grasses.<ref name="POWOgenus">{{cite web |title=''Phleum'' L. |website=Plants of the World Online |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:18725-1 |access-date=2026-03-14}}</ref>

==Description==
''Phleum bertolonii'' grows to {{convert|50|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall, rarely {{convert|70|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall, with [[leaf|leaves]] {{convert|2|–|6|mm|1|abbr=on}} broad. The leaves have short, narrow, acute-tipped [[ligule]]s.<ref name="Streeter">{{cite book |last=Streeter |first=David |title=Flower Guide |publisher=Collins |publication-place=London |date=2010 |isbn=978-0-00-718389-0 |pages=632–633}}</ref><ref name="Fitter">{{cite book |last1=Fitter |first1=Richard Sidney Richmond |last2=Fitter |first2=Alastair |title=Collins Guide to the Grasses, Sedges, Rushes, and Ferns of Britain and Northern Europe |publisher=Harpercollins Pub Limited |publication-place=London |date=1984 |isbn=0-00-219136-9 |pages=98–99}}</ref>

The [[flower]]head is up to {{convert|8|cm|0|abbr=on}} long and {{convert|3|–|7.5|mm|1|abbr=on}} broad, with densely packed spikelets, the [[glume]]s with a 0.2–1.2&nbsp;mm long awn. It flowers from June until August.<ref name="Streeter"/><ref name="Fitter"/>

It has often been confused with [[Timothy (grass)|Timothy]] (''Phleum pratense''); this is most easily distinguished by its broad, blunt ligules, and is a much larger plant, growing to 150&nbsp;cm, 2–3 times as tall as ''P. bertolonii'' and with flowerheads twice as long.<ref name="PlantAtlas"/><ref name="Streeter"/><ref name="Fitter"/>

==Taxonomy==
In the past, it was often considered a [[subspecies]] of ''Phleum pratense'', as ''Phleum pratense'' subsp. ''bertolonii'',<ref name="Fitter"/> but is now generally considered to be a separate species.<ref name="POWO"/><ref name="PlantAtlas"/><ref name="Streeter"/>

